  "post": {
    "caption": "Named for a daughter and the sea. Larimar is a blue variety of the mineral pectolite, and traces of copper are believed to color its sky blue. It is known only from the Dominican Republic. Father Miguel Domingo Fuertes is credited with finding it in 1916, and then it was forgotten for decades. A Peace Corps volunteer and Miguel Méndez rediscovered it in 1974, and blue pebbles on the coast reportedly led searchers upriver to the source. Much of it is dug by hand in narrow shafts and tunnels. Méndez combined his daughter Larissa's name with mar, Spanish for sea. Larimar is soft, so keep it away from heat and harsh chemicals.",
